.TH SHTL 1 "2026-11-01" "shtl 2.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shtl \- save directories as routes with short names, then go to them by name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shtl
.I NAME
.RI [ SUBDIR ]
.br
.B shtl
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shtl
saves directories as routes.
A route is a short name for a directory.
To go to the directory, enter the route name.
.PP
A program cannot change the directory of the shell that started it, so
.B shtl
has a shell function that does the
.BR cd .
Without the shell function,
.B shtl
.I NAME
prints the path and a warning.
Refer to
.BR "SHELL FUNCTION" .
.PP
With no arguments,
.B shtl
prints the help text.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBshtl\fR \fINAME\fR [\fISUBDIR\fR]
Go to a route, or to a folder in it.
.I SUBDIR
is relative to the route.
.TP
\fBadd\fR \fINAME\fR [\fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-p\fR | \fB\-\-persist\fR]
Add a route for this host.
.I PATH
must be absolute, or start with
.BR ~/ .
The default is the current directory.
The directory must exist, unless you use
.BR \-\-persist .
A persistent route can go to a directory that does not exist yet, and
.B clean
does not remove it.
.TP
\fBupdate\fR \fINAME\fR [\fIPATH\fR]
Change the path of a route on this host.
.I PATH
is the same as for
.BR add .
The route keeps its
.B persist
value.
.TP
\fBremove\fR | \fBrm\fR \fINAME\fR
Remove a route from this host.
.TP
\fBlist\fR | \fBls\fR [\fB\-\-names\fR]
Show the routes of this host, sorted by name.
A
.B *
marks the route that you are in, and
.B (persist)
marks a persistent route.
.B \-\-names
prints only the names, for scripts and tab completion.
.TP
\fBclean\fR
Show a checklist of the routes of this host whose directories do not exist, then remove the selected routes.
It does not show persistent routes or routes without
.BR hosts .
This command needs a terminal.
In the checklist, the arrow keys or
.B j
and
.B k
move,
.B space
toggles a route,
.B a
toggles all routes,
.B enter
removes the selected routes, and
.B q
cancels.
.TP
\fBpersist\fR \fINAME\fR
Turn
.B persist
on or off for a route on this host.
To turn it off, the directory must exist.
.TP
\fBpath\fR \fINAME\fR [\fISUBDIR\fR]
Print the path of a route, or of a folder in it.
The directory must exist.
.TP
\fBdoctor\fR
Print the name of this host, and the problems in the routes file with their line numbers.
It only reports: it does not change the file.
.TP
\fBsetup\fR
Add the shell function to the rc file of your shell.
It shows the change and asks first.
.TP
\fBunsetup\fR
Remove the shell function from your rc files.
It shows the change and asks first.
.TP
\fBinit\fR
Print the shell function and the tab completion for bash and zsh.
.TP
\fBhelp\fR
Print the help text.
.TP
\fBversion\fR
Print the version.
.SH SHELL FUNCTION
The shell function does the
.B cd
and gives tab completion.
.B shtl setup
adds the lines for your shell to your rc file.
To add them manually, put them at the end of the file.
.PP
For bash:
.PP
.EX
# shtl: https://github.com/bitmvr-industries/shtl
shtl() { unset \-f shtl; eval "$(command shtl init 2>/dev/null)"; shtl "$@"; }; _shtl() { local s; s="$(command shtl init 2>/dev/null)" || return; unset \-f _shtl; eval "$s"; _shtl "$@"; }; complete \-F _shtl shtl
.EE
.PP
For zsh, put them after
.BR compinit :
.PP
.EX
# shtl: https://github.com/bitmvr-industries/shtl
shtl() { unset \-f shtl; eval "$(command shtl init 2>/dev/null)"; shtl "$@"; }; _shtl() { local s; s="$(command shtl init 2>/dev/null)" || return; unset \-f _shtl; eval "$s"; _shtl "$@"; }; if (( $+functions[compdef] )); then compdef _shtl shtl; fi
.EE
.PP
These lines do not start
.B shtl
until you use it.
Thus, a line that changes your
.B PATH
can be after these lines.
.PP
.B setup
selects the rc file from your shell:
.TP
zsh
.I $ZDOTDIR/.zshrc
or
.I ~/.zshrc
.TP
bash on Linux
.I ~/.bashrc
.TP
bash on macOS
The first of
.IR ~/.bash_profile ,
.IR ~/.bash_login ,
.IR ~/.profile .
If that file reads
.IR ~/.bashrc ,
then
.IR ~/.bashrc .
.SH ROUTES FILE
Routes are in a TOML file:
.PP
.EX
version = 2

[[route]]
name = "downloads"
path = "~/Downloads"
hosts = ["mbp"]

[[route]]
name = "downloads"
path = "/var/tmp/downloads"
hosts = ["midgard"]

[[route]]
name = "nas"
path = "/Volumes/nas"
persist = true
.EE
.TP
.B version
The version of the file format. It must be 2.
.TP
.B name
A name must not be empty, start with
.BR \- ,
contain white space or control characters, or be a
.B shtl
command.
.TP
.B path
An absolute path.
.B ~
is the home directory of the computer.
.TP
.B hosts
Optional. The computers that use the route.
.TP
.B persist
Optional. If true, the route can go to a directory that does not exist, and
.B clean
does not remove it.
.PP
.B shtl
keeps comments, blank lines, and the sequence of the routes.
If you change the file in a text editor, run
.B shtl doctor
to find problems.
.SH HOSTS
One routes file can operate on many computers, for example in a dotfiles repository.
.PP
A route with
.B hosts
operates only on these computers.
A route without
.B hosts
operates on all computers.
.B shtl
does not make these routes: you add them in a text editor.
If a route for this host and a route without
.B hosts
have the same name,
.B shtl
uses the route for this host.
A new computer starts with no routes, because each route that
.B shtl
adds has
.BR hosts .
.PP
The host name is the LocalHostName on macOS, and the host name before the first
.B .\&
on Linux.
Case does not matter.
.B shtl doctor
shows the host name.
.PP
A command changes the routes of this host only:
.TP
.B add
Adds this host to
.BR hosts .
If a route has the same name, path, and
.B persist
value,
.B add
puts this host in that route.
.TP
.BR update ", " persist
Change the route if this host is the only host in it.
If the route has other hosts,
.B shtl
removes this host from the route and adds a route for this host.
.TP
.B remove
Removes this host from
.BR hosts .
If no hosts stay, it deletes the route.
It deletes a route without
.B hosts
on all computers.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
If set, the routes file is in this directory instead of
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.B HOME
The home directory, for
.B ~
in paths and for the default routes file and rc files.
.TP
.B ZDOTDIR
If set,
.B setup
and
.B unsetup
use the zsh rc file in this directory.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/shtl/routes.toml
The routes file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error, or
.B doctor
found a problem.
.TP
.B 130
You pressed Ctrl\-C in the
.B clean
checklist.
.PP
Errors, warnings, and information messages go to standard error.
.B doctor
reports problems on standard output.
.SH EXAMPLES
Save the current directory as
.B work
and go to it:
.PP
.EX
shtl add work
shtl work
.EE
.PP
Go to a folder in a route:
.PP
.EX
shtl work code/api
.EE
.PP
Copy a file to a route:
.PP
.EX
cp file "$(shtl path work)"
.EE
.PP
Add a route to a disk that is not mounted yet:
.PP
.EX
shtl add nas /Volumes/nas \-\-persist
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cd (1)
.PP
https://github.com/bitmvr\-industries/shtl
